---
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Jason Scott - network engineer building autonomous systems."
	pubDate={new Date('July 25 2026')}
>
	<p>
		I'm Jason Scott, a network engineer. A good chunk of my work is enterprise wireless and RF - link
		budgets, spectrum planning, and the unglamorous discipline of making radios coexist reliably in
		crowded airspace - but networking is bigger than any one band, and so is the day job. I'm
		working toward my <a href="https://www.cwnp.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CWNP</a>
		certification, and I spend a lot of time thinking about the physical layer most people never see.
	</p>

	<p>
		FlightLab is where that background collides with a hobby: building an AI-piloted drone from the
		ground up. It turns out a drone is a wonderful excuse to work across the whole stack at once -
		RF and networking, embedded Linux and edge AI, real-time computer vision, closed-loop control,
		and flight-controller integration. The interesting problems live in the seams between those
		subsystems.
	</p>

	<p>
		A theme you'll see throughout: <strong>systems integration done with a safety-first mindset.</strong>
		Independent subsystems have to work together, and the real engineering is making sure no single
		one of them is ever allowed to do something dumb - fail-safe defaults, clear ownership of who
		decides what, and testing the scary changes in simulation before they touch hardware. That
		instinct comes straight from aviation, and it's the part I care about most.
	</p>

	<p>
		If you're working on autonomous systems, edge robotics, or the networks that hold them together,
		I'd love to talk. You can find my code on <a
			href="https://github.com/JasonScottSF"
			target="_blank"
			rel="noopener">GitHub</a
		>.
	</p>
</Layout>
